1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,020
These are two browser runs against the same
release of Fieldnotes.

2
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:08,520
On the left, a new user wrote a draft, saved it,
and opened it again.

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:12,340
On the right, a returning user arrived with a
draft from yesterday.

4
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:13,720
Both fields are empty.

5
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:16,060
This is a deliberately broken example.

6
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:18,440
The question Returner asks is simple.

7
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:22,720
Will yesterday's work survive today's release?

8
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:26,520
From the Returner repository, run npm run
demo:repair.

9
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:31,080
With a colon between demo and repair, the command
starts a local file server and gives

10
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:33,120
Kane two versions of the app.

11
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:34,480
Kane uses real Chrome.

12
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:37,260
Each run gets an isolated test profile.

13
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:42,000
You can see the browser checkpoints in this
recording, taken from the actual Kane evidence

14
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:43,720
packs.

15
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:46,920
First, Kane opens version 1.

16
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:52,720
It types a fresh title and body, saves them,
reloads the page, and checks both values.

17
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:58,520
The randomly chosen title ends in F939. Watch that
same title come back later.

18
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:03,360
Returner keeps a copy of this closed browser
profile so the repair can be tested from exactly

19
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,560
where this user started.

20
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:10,120
Now Returner serves version 2 at the same local
address.

21
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:14,040
Changing the port would change the browser's
storage origin and spoil the test.

22
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:17,440
A fresh profile can still create and reopen a
draft.

23
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:20,280
Then Kane opens a copy of the old profile.

24
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:25,560
Its instruction is to find the existing draft, and
to stop immediately if it is missing.

25
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:29,960
Typing the missing text back in would hide the
regression.

26
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:31,420
Here is what Kane observed.

27
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:33,660
The title and body fields were empty.

28
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:38,220
That observation, the unchanged objective, and the
earlier release's storage code go

29
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:40,280
directly to Codex.

30
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:43,360
Codex works on a disposable copy of the candidate
app.

31
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:47,380
In this run it found that version 2 looked only
under its new storage key.

32
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:52,400
It changed the lookup so an existing version 2
draft takes priority, and an older draft

33
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:55,400
can still be opened.

34
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:57,800
The agent has finished its patch.

35
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:59,860
Returner now makes the release decision.

36
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:03,220
It starts again from the untouched version 1
profile.

37
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:08,620
Kane finds the original title and body, appends
the word edited, saves, and reloads.

38
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:11,880
Then Returner closes Chrome and opens another
process.

39
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:13,660
This last check only reads.

40
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:20,880
On screen is the same draft, with its edited body
still present. All three checks passed.

41
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:23,980
Fieldnotes shows the repair on a defect we
introduced.

42
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:28,980
For a separate confirmation, we built two
published releases of DeckMark, an open source

43
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:30,440
Markdown editor.

44
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:35,920
Kane created a note on version 2.5.3. On version
2.5.4,

45
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:41,320
it opened that note, edited it, and confirmed the
original content and the edit after reload.

46
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:47,000
This result applies to those two releases and this
particular editing task.

47
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,440
The public repository includes the command, the
example, the agent patch workflow, and

48
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:54,000
the recorded results.

49
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,380
You can open the reports locally even without
running Kane.

50
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:02,300
To rehearse your own static app, point Returner at
two build directories and describe the promise

51
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,440
your returning user depends on.

52
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:08,700
The two users can see the same release and have
very different experiences.

53
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:09,920
Now you can test both.
